<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <base href="/" />
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    @* No static <title> here: every page supplies its own through <PageTitle>, and with
       prerendering the HeadOutlet emits a real <title> element into this head. A second,
       earlier one would win in the served HTML (browsers and crawlers take the first). *@
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="description" content="bit Belemetry - one lightweight, OpenTelemetry-native telemetry SDK for every Blazor app: page views, web vitals, errors, fetch spans, ILogger, Activity and Meter, exported over OTLP or to Azure Monitor, with per-feature GDPR consent." />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
    @* Painted behind the browser's own chrome on the platforms that colour it. Two declarations,
       one per scheme, so the bar matches the theme the page is actually in - the values are the
       --bl-clr-bg-1 of each theme in theme.css, which a meta tag cannot read a variable for. *@
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#FFFFFF" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#1F1F1F" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />

    @* What a link to the site looks like when it is shared. *@
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="bit Belemetry" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="bit Belemetry - telemetry for every Blazor app" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="Browser and .NET telemetry for Blazor, OpenTelemetry-native without the OpenTelemetry packages: OTLP, Azure Monitor, per-feature consent." />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://belemetry.bitplatform.dev" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />

    <link rel="icon" href="favicon.ico" type="image/x-icon" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="512x512" href="icon-512.png" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/theme.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/app.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/docs.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/belemetry.css" />

    @* The theme is applied here, in the head, rather than from js/demo.js at the end of the body:
       the stylesheets key off the data-theme attribute, and setting it after the first paint would
       show the reader a flash of the other theme. This is the same decision js/demo.js makes for
       the app's lifetime - see window.blTheme there - kept short enough to inline. *@
    <script>
        (function () {
            var stored = null;
            try { stored = localStorage.getItem('bl-theme'); } catch (e) { /* no stored preference */ }
            var theme = (stored === 'light' || stored === 'dark')
                ? stored
                : (window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches ? 'dark' : 'light');
            document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', theme);
        })();
    </script>

    @* Belemetry's browser script, as early as possible: it starts before Blazor boots, so it sees the
       initial page load, the web vitals and any early error. It writes its configuration (built from
       the options registered in Program.cs) into the page and loads _content/Bit.Belemetry/bit-belemetry.js.
       <BelemetryInitializer /> in Routes.razor connects it to .NET once the app is interactive. *@
    <BelemetryScript />

    @* Same render mode as the app itself, so <PageTitle> keeps working after hydration. *@
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
</head>

<body>
    @* Prerendered on the server, then hydrated by the WebAssembly runtime. The .NET half of Belemetry
       runs in the browser from then on; the prerender pass only renders. See Layout/MainLayout.razor
       for the RendererInfo-driven hydration hint. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />

    <div id="blazor-error-ui">
        An unhandled error has occurred.
        <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
        <span class="dismiss">&#x1F5D9;</span>
    </div>

    @* Loaded before the Blazor script so the window.bl* helpers exist by the time the app boots. *@
    <script src="js/demo.js"></script>
    <script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"></script>
</body>

</html>
